Executive Summary

The QUENNE Hybrid Intelligent Star Ship (Q-HISS) proposes a next-generation deep-space platform built on stacked intelligence architecture, integrating photonic processing, neuromorphic cognition, quantum-assisted optimization, and human-governed autonomy.

Future space missions require platforms capable of:
   •   Multi-year autonomous operation
   •   Navigation beyond Earth infrastructure
   •   Self-diagnosis and adaptive resilience
   •   Intelligent resource management
   •   Human-AI cooperative control

Q-HISS addresses these requirements through layered intelligence systems enabling machine-speed perception while preserving human authority and mission governance.

The architecture extends QUENNE intelligence from atmospheric systems and orbital platforms into deep-space infrastructure.

⸻

1. Introduction

Current spacecraft architectures remain:
   •   Heavily Earth-dependent
   •   Limited in onboard decision autonomy
   •   Vulnerable to communication delays
   •   Resource inefficient over long missions

Deep-space missions introduce new challenges:
   •   Communication delays of minutes to hours
   •   Harsh radiation environments
   •   Long-duration hardware degradation
   •   Energy constraints
   •   Unknown mission conditions

Q-HISS introduces cognitive spacecraft architecture, where intelligence becomes a core system component rather than an auxiliary function.

